{"records":[{"id":"835fcfbvhs","title":"Preparation of CRISPR/Cas9 and gRNA plasmids","description":"Human genome engineering has been transformed by the introduction of the CRISPR (clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats)/Cas (CRISPR-associated) system found in most bacteria and archaea. Type II CRISPR/Cas systems have been engineered to induce RNA-guided genome editing in human cells, where small RNAs function together with Cas9 nucleases for sequence-specific cleavage of target sequences. Here we describe the protocol for Cas9-mediated human genome engineering, including construct building and transfection methods necessary for delivering Cas9 and guide RNA (gRNA) into human-induced pluripotent stem cells (hiPSCs) and HEK293 cells. Following genome editing, we also describe methods to assess genome editing effi- ciency using next-generation sequencing and isolate monoclonal hiPSCs with the desired modifications for downstream applications. Keywords: genome engineering • CRISPR • human stem cells","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2016-12-12T12:58:15.307Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/835fcfbvhs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/835fcfbvhs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"George","last_name":"Church","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Method"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4g722x4jfg","title":"Western blot","description":"Western blot of CRISPR-Cas9-mediated nontargeting (NT) versus BACH2 knockout 3 days after CRISPR-Cas9-gRNA nucleofection of human gut cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-02T21:20:55.966Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4g722x4jfg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4g722x4jfg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yulong","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gut","Western Blot","T Cell","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9w4g54bv43","title":"Genome-wide screens reveal that resveratrol induces replicative stress in human cells","description":"Resveratrol is a natural product associated with wide-ranging effects in animal and cellular models including lifespan extension. To identify the genetic target of resveratrol in human cells, we conducted genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screens to pinpoint genes that confer sensitivity or resistance to resveratrol. An extensive network of DNA damage response and replicative stress genes exhibited genetic interactions with resveratrol and its analog pterostilbene. These genetic profiles showed similarity to the response to hydroxyurea, an inhibitor of ribonucleotide reductase that causes replicative stress. Resveratrol, pterostilbene and hydroxyurea caused similar depletion of nucleotide pools, inhibition of replication fork progression and induction of replicative stress. The ability of resveratrol to inhibit cell proliferation and S phase transit was independent of the histone deacetylase Sirtuin 1, which has been implicated in lifespan extension by resveratrol. These results establish that a primary impact of resveratrol on human cell proliferation is the induction of low-level replicative stress.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-04T14:56:19.488Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9w4g54bv43","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9w4g54bv43"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yahya","last_name":"Benslimane","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thierry","last_name":"Bertomeu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jasmin","last_name":"Coulombe-Huntington","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mary","last_name":"McQuaid","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Sánchez-Osuna","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David","last_name":"Papadopoli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daina","last_name":"Avizonis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mariana","last_name":"De Sa Tavares Russo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Caroline","last_name":"Huard","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ivan","last_name":"Topisirovic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hugo","last_name":"Wurtele","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mike","last_name":"Tyers","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lea","last_name":"Harrington","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Replication","High-Throughput Screening","High-Throughput Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nknd6w2kbm","title":"Supplementary information's for accepted manuscript in Cell press \"Star protocols\" :A Simple Method to Isolate a Single Human Cell PRDX1 Knockout Generated by CRISPR-Cas9 System","description":"Revised Supplementary information's for accepted manuscript in Cell press \"Star protocols\" :A Simple Method to Isolate a Single Human Cell PRDX1 Knockout Generated by CRISPR-Cas9 System","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-09T15:20:06.778Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nknd6w2kbm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nknd6w2kbm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mustapha","last_name":"Aouida","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Life Sciences","Cell Biology","Genetic Engineering","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6wm46g4tw","title":"Data set for the paper entitled \"CRISPR-enabled genetic screens identify synthetic lethal targets across frequently altered cancer drivers\" by Desjardins, Bowlan, et al. (2026)","description":"We undertook a series of genome-wide CRISPR screens using functionally validated isogenic cell lines and mapped SL interactions across 15 genetic alterations characteristic of diseases with high incidence and unmet clinical need: FBXW7, CCNE1, CDK12, ARID1A, KMT2D, DNMT3A, TET2, KEAP1, STK11, IDH1, SF3B1, SRSF2, U2AF1, chromosome 18q loss, and chromosome 13q loss. The data deposited here make the raw read count files available for all CRISPR screens conducted in the study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T11:43:11.208Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6wm46g4tw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6wm46g4tw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Álvarez-Quilón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Desjardins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Bowlan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6bkjdg5g85","title":"Zmat3 is a key splicing regulator in the p53 tumor suppression program","description":"Although TP53 is the most commonly mutated gene in human cancers, the p53-dependent transcriptional programs mediating tumor suppression remain incompletely understood. Here, to uncover critical components downstream of p53 in tumor suppression, we perform unbiased RNAi and CRISPR/Cas9-based genetic screens in vivo.  These screens converge upon the p53-inducible gene Zmat3, encoding an RNA-binding-protein, and we demonstrate that ZMAT3 is an important tumor suppressor downstream of p53 in mouse KrasG12D-driven lung and liver cancers and human carcinomas. Integrative analysis of the ZMAT3 RNA-binding landscape and transcriptomic profiling reveals that ZMAT3 directly modulates exon inclusion in transcripts encoding proteins of diverse functions, including the p53 inhibitors MDM4 and MDM2, splicing regulators, and components of varied cellular processes. Interestingly, these exons are enriched in NMD signals, and, accordingly, ZMAT3 broadly affects target transcript stability. Collectively, these studies reveal ZMAT3 as a novel RNA-splicing and homeostasis regulator and key component of p53-mediated tumor suppression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T16:30:19.918Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6bkjdg5g85","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6bkjdg5g85"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kathryn","last_name":"Bieging-Rolett","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Tumor","Alternative Splicing","RNA Sequencing","P53","Mouse Model of Cancer","Adenocarcinoma","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nb9hh8yyds","title":"Cross-regulation between TDP-43 and paraspeckles promotes pluripotency-differentiation transition","description":"additional supplementary data:\n- Sanger sequencing and confirmatory gel electrophoresis confirming the CRISPR-Cas9 edited human and mouse embryonic stem cells presented in the manuscript under review.\n\n- additional supplementary data: raw unprocessed confocal images presented in Fig2 of the manuscript under review: ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-03-18T19:45:41.433Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nb9hh8yyds","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nb9hh8yyds"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Miha","last_name":"Modic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jernej","last_name":"Ule","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Micha","last_name":"Drukker","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Sequencing","Western Blot","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hbr8p7n526","title":"Cross-regulation between TDP-43 and paraspeckles promotes pluripotency-differentiation transition. Modic et al","description":"Sanger sequencing and confirmatory gel electrophoresis confirming the CRISPR-Cas9 edited human and mouse embryonic stem cells presented in the manuscript under review entitled \"Cross-regulation between TDP-43 and paraspeckles promotes pluripotency-differentiation transition\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-03-19T17:58:48.63Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hbr8p7n526","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hbr8p7n526"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"micha","last_name":"drukker","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["DNA Sequencing","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":130912,"page":1,"page_size":1